<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Assignment 10: Extreme Values and Saddle Points, and Lagrange Multipliers.

  The odd-numbered problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for Extreme
  Values and Saddle Points" (ex-extrema-triangle, rm2-ex-2, rw21-7, rw23-4
  and pp-35), which lived inline in exercises.ptx, followed by the
  odd-numbered problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for Lagrange
  Multipliers" (exer-parallel-tangent-plane, exer-cobb-douglas, rf-7,
  rs19-10, pp-37 and pp-39), which lived in lagrange_exercise.ptx.  The
  even-numbered problems of both went to review-problems-4.ptx.

  ex-extrema-triangle carried an interactive step-by-step hint (the
  "hints-extrema-triangle" iframe and its print-only note); both the markup
  and the asset assets/iframe/hint-extrema-triangle.html were deleted at the
  instructor's request.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-10" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 10</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems find absolute extrema on a closed region, classify critical
points with the second derivative test, and use Lagrange multipliers to
optimize a function subject to a constraint. If you would like to review the
material first, see
<xref ref="section-extreme-values" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="section-lagrange-multipliers" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-extrema-triangle">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of
            <md>f(x,y)=x^2-xy+y^2-3y</md>
            on the region bounded by the <m>x</m>- and
            <m>y</m>-axes and the line <m>x+y=4</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>

         <solution>
      <p>
        The gradient is <m>\nabla f = \langle 2x - y,\; -x + 2y - 3\rangle</m>, which
        vanishes only at <m>(1,2)</m>, a point in the interior of the region. There
        <m>f(1,2) = -3</m>. Since this is the only critical point on all of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> and it is a minimum (by the second derivative test), it is a
        global minimum. We now examine the three boundary segments to look for possible
        maxima.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>x</m>-axis with <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(x,0) = x^2</m>, which
        is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>y</m>-axis with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(0,y) = y^2 - 3y</m>,
        which is maximized at <m>f(0,4) = 4</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, on the line <m>x = 4 - y</m> with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have
        <m>f(4-y,\,y) = 3y^2 - 15y + 16</m>, which is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Altogether, there is an absolute minimum <m>f(1,2) = -3</m> and an absolute maximum
        <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
      </exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-ex-2">
<statement>
<p>
Find and classify all the critical points of
<m>f(x,y) = xy\,e^{x^2+y^2}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
\nabla f(x,y) = \left\langle
\left(1 + 2x^2\right) y\, e^{x^2+y^2},\;
x \left(1 + 2y^2\right) e^{x^2+y^2}
\right\rangle.
</md>
Since the exponential factors, as well as <m>1 + 2x^2</m> and
<m>1 + 2y^2</m>, are always positive, we might as well ignore them
when finding the zeros. So we are left with
<m>\langle x, y\rangle = \langle 0, 0\rangle</m>, i.e. the only
critical point is <m>(0,0)</m>. The second derivatives of <m>f</m> are
<md>
<mrow>f_{xx} \amp = \left(6x + 4x^3\right) y\, e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>f_{xy} \amp = \left(1 + 2x^2\right)\left(1 + 2y^2\right)
e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>f_{yy} \amp = x\left(6y + 4y^3\right) e^{x^2+y^2}</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>D(0,0) = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = -1</m>, so <m>(0,0)</m> is
a saddle point.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-7">
<statement>
<p>
Find the maximum and minimum values of the function
<m>f(x,y) = 2x^2 + 3y^2 - 4x - 5</m> on the domain
<m>x^2 + y^2 \le 121</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<em>Critical points in the interior:</em>
<md>
f_x = 4x - 4 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 1,
\qquad
f_y = 6y = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = 0,
</md>
so <m>(1,0)</m> is the only critical point, with
<m>f(1,0) = -7</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>The boundary:</em> on the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 121</m> we have
<m>y^2 = 121 - x^2</m> with <m>-11 \le x \le 11</m>, so
<md>
f = 2x^2 + 3\left(121 - x^2\right) - 4x - 5
= 358 - 4x - x^2,
</md>
and
<md>
\frac{d}{dx}\left(358 - 4x - x^2\right) = -4 - 2x = 0
\;\Rightarrow\; x = -2,
</md>
giving <m>y^2 = 117</m>, i.e. <m>y = \pm\sqrt{117}</m>, with
<md>
f\left(-2, \pm\sqrt{117}\right) = 358 + 8 - 4 = 362.
</md>
At the endpoints <m>x = \pm 11</m> (where <m>y = 0</m>),
<m>f(11,0) = 193</m> and <m>f(-11,0) = 281</m>.
</p>

<p>
Comparing all the values, the maximum value of <m>f</m> is
<m>362</m>, attained at <m>\left(-2, \sqrt{117}\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-2, -\sqrt{117}\right)</m>, and the minimum value is
<m>-7</m>, attained at <m>(1,0)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-rw21-disk"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw21-disk">
<caption>
The disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 121</m>, the interior minimum at
<m>(1,0)</m>, and the boundary maxima at
<m>\left(-2, \pm\sqrt{117}\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rw21-disk">
<diagram dimensions="(300,290)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (11*cos(t), 11*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-15.5,-14.5,15.5,14.5)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="dk-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dk-min" p="(1,0)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="dk-max1" p="(-2,10.8167)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dk-max2" p="(-2,-10.8167)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="dk-min-lab" anchor="(1.6,-2.6)" alignment="se" color="#009E73">
<m>(1,0),\ f=-7</m>
</label>
<label at="dk-max1-lab" anchor="(-14.8,12.1)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\left(-2,\sqrt{117}\right),\ f=362</m>
</label>
<label at="dk-max2-lab" anchor="(-14.8,-13.2)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\left(-2,-\sqrt{117}\right),\ f=362</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue circle of radius eleven centered at the origin. A green point just right of the center at one comma zero marks the minimum, f equals negative seven. Two vermillion points on the circle at negative two comma plus and minus the square root of one hundred seventeen mark the maxima, f equals three hundred sixty two.">
  <annotation ref="dk-circle" text="The boundary circle x squared plus y squared equals one hundred twenty one."/>
  <annotation ref="dk-min" text="The interior critical point one comma zero, where the minimum negative seven occurs."/>
  <annotation ref="dk-max1" text="A boundary maximum at negative two comma square root of one hundred seventeen."/>
  <annotation ref="dk-max2" text="A boundary maximum at negative two comma negative square root of one hundred seventeen."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-4">
<statement>
<p>
The business manager for Acme Corporation plots a grid on a map of the
region Acme serves and determines that the company's three most
important customers are located at points <m>A(1,5)</m>,
<m>B(0,0)</m>, and <m>C(8,0)</m>, where units are in miles. At what
point <m>W(x,y)</m> should a warehouse be located in order to minimize
<em>the sum of the squares of the distances</em> (NOT the square of
the sum) from <m>W</m> to <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The sum of the squares of the distances is
<md>
<mrow>f(x,y) \amp = (x-1)^2 + (y-5)^2 + x^2 + y^2 + (x-8)^2 + y^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 3x^2 - 18x + 3y^2 - 10y + 90.</mrow>
</md>
Setting the partial derivatives equal to zero,
<md>
<mrow>f_x \amp = 6x - 18 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 3,</mrow>
<mrow>f_y \amp = 6y - 10 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = \frac53,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>\left(3, \frac53\right)</m> is the only critical point. The
second derivatives are <m>f_{xx} = 6 \gt 0</m>, <m>f_{yy} = 6</m>,
<m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 36 \gt 0</m>. Since <m>D \gt 0</m>
and <m>f_{xx} \gt 0</m>, the point <m>\left(3, \frac53\right)</m> is a
local minimum.
</p>

<p>
Also, if we complete the squares we can see that this point is where
the absolute minimum happens:
<md>
f(x,y) = 3\left(y - \frac53\right)^2 + 3(x-3)^2
+ \underbrace{90 - \frac{25}{3} - 27}_{\gt 0},
</md>
where the first two terms are non-negative and vanish exactly at
<m>\left(3, \frac53\right)</m>. See <xref ref="fig-rw23-warehouse"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw23-warehouse">
<caption>
The three customers <m>A(1,5)</m>, <m>B(0,0)</m>, <m>C(8,0)</m>, and
the warehouse location <m>W\left(3, \frac53\right)</m> that minimizes
the sum of the squares of the distances.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rw23-warehouse">
<diagram dimensions="(360,260)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.2,9.2,6.2)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line at="wh-wa" endpoints="((3,1.6667),(1,5))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="wh-wb" endpoints="((3,1.6667),(0,0))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="wh-wc" endpoints="((3,1.6667),(8,0))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="5 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="wh-A" p="(1,5)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="wh-B" p="(0,0)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="wh-C" p="(8,0)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="wh-W" p="(3,1.6667)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="wh-A-lab" anchor="(1.15,5.15)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
<m>A(1,5)</m>
</label>
<label at="wh-B-lab" anchor="(0.15,-0.35)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>B(0,0)</m>
</label>
<label at="wh-C-lab" anchor="(7.9,-0.35)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2">
<m>C(8,0)</m>
</label>
<label at="wh-W-lab" anchor="(3.25,1.85)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>W\!\left(3,\frac53\right)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three blue points mark the customers: A at one comma five, B at the origin, and C at eight comma zero. A vermillion point W at three comma five thirds sits between them, joined to each customer by a dashed green segment.">
  <annotation ref="wh-W" text="The warehouse location W at three comma five thirds, the point minimizing the sum of the squares of the distances."/>
  <annotation ref="wh-A" text="Customer A at one comma five."/>
  <annotation ref="wh-B" text="Customer B at the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="wh-C" text="Customer C at eight comma zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-35">
<statement>
<p>
Find all local maxima, minima and saddle points for
<m>f(x,y) = \frac13 x^3 - x + xy^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The first partials are
<md>
f_x = x^2 - 1 + y^2,
\qquad
f_y = 2xy.
</md>
Setting <m>f_y = 0</m> forces <m>x = 0</m> or <m>y = 0</m>.
<ul>
<li>
<p>
If <m>x = 0</m>: <m>f_x = -1 + y^2 = 0</m> gives <m>y = \pm 1</m>, so
the critical points are <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(0,-1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If <m>y = 0</m>: <m>f_x = x^2 - 1 = 0</m> gives <m>x = \pm 1</m>, so
the critical points are <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(-1,0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
The second partials are <m>f_{xx} = 2x</m>, <m>f_{yy} = 2x</m>, and
<m>f_{xy} = 2y</m>, so the discriminant is
<md>
D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^{2} = 4x^2 - 4y^2.
</md>
Applying the second derivative test:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>(0, \pm 1)</m>: <m>D = -4 \lt 0</m>, so both are
<term>saddle points</term>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>(1,0)</m>: <m>D = 4 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} = 2 \gt 0</m>, so this
is a <term>local minimum</term>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>(-1,0)</m>: <m>D = 4 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} = -2 \lt 0</m>, so
this is a <term>local maximum</term>.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-parallel-tangent-plane">
      <title>Tangent Planes Parallel to a Given Plane</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the points on the surface
          <me>x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 = 1</me>
          at which the tangent plane is parallel to the plane
          <m>-x - 5y - 2z = -3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Multiplying by <m>-1</m>, the given plane is
          <m>x + 5y + 2z = 3</m>, with normal vector
          <m>\mathbf{n} = \langle 1, 5, 2\rangle</m>.
          The tangent plane to the ellipsoid is parallel to this plane exactly
          at the points of the ellipsoid that are closest to and farthest from
          it, so we minimize and maximize the distance to the plane subject to
          the constraint of lying on the ellipsoid.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-schematic">
          <caption>
            A schematic cross-section.  The tangent plane to the ellipsoid is
            parallel to the given plane exactly at the closest point <m>S_1</m>
            and the farthest point <m>S_2</m>, where <m>\nabla g</m> is
            parallel to the plane's normal <m>\mathbf{n}</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-lagrange-schematic" width="75%">
            <prefigure label="lagrange-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(500,330)" margins="5">
                <definition>S1 = (sqrt(2), sqrt(2)/2)</definition>
                <definition>S2 = (-sqrt(2), -sqrt(2)/2)</definition>
                <definition>P = (2.5, 1)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="[-3.6,-2.3,5.6,3.3]">

                  <group at="constraint">
                    <ellipse at="ellipse" center="(0,0)" axes="(2,1)"
                             stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="constraint-label" anchor="(0.4,-1.12)" alignment="southeast">
                      <m>g(x,y,z)=0</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="plane">
                    <line at="plane-line" p1="(4.9,-0.2)" p2="(-0.1,2.3)"
                          stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="plane-label" anchor="(0.8,2.35)" alignment="north">
                      <m>x+5y+2z=3</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="tangencies">
                    <group at="tangency-S1">
                      <line at="tangent-S1" p1="S1" p2="S1+(2,-1)" infinite="yes"
                            stroke="gray" dash="9 5"/>
                      <vector at="gradg-S1" v="(0.65,1.3)" tail="S1"
                              stroke="orange" thickness="3"/>
                      <label at="gradg-S1-label" anchor="S1+(0.65,1.3)" alignment="west">
                        <m>\nabla g</m>
                      </label>
                      <point at="S1-point" p="S1" fill="red" alignment="southwest">
                        <m>S_1</m>
                      </point>
                    </group>
                    <group at="tangency-S2">
                      <line at="tangent-S2" p1="S2" p2="S2+(2,-1)" infinite="yes"
                            stroke="gray" dash="9 5"/>
                      <vector at="gradg-S2" v="(-0.65,-1.3)" tail="S2"
                              stroke="orange" thickness="3"/>
                      <label at="gradg-S2-label" anchor="S2+(-0.65,-1.3)" alignment="southeast">
                        <m>\nabla g</m>
                      </label>
                      <point at="S2-point" p="S2" fill="red" alignment="northeast">
                        <m>S_2</m>
                      </point>
                    </group>
                  </group>

                  <group at="distance">
                    <line at="distance-segment" p1="S1" p2="P"
                          stroke="red" dash="4 4" thickness="2"/>
                  </group>

                  <group at="normal">
                    <vector at="n-vector" v="(0.55,1.1)" tail="P"
                            stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="n-label" anchor="P+(0.55,1.1)" alignment="northeast">
                      <m>\mathbf{n}=\langle 1,5,2\rangle</m>
                    </label>
                    <point at="P-point" p="P" fill="black" alignment="east">
                      <m>P(3,0,0)</m>
                    </point>
                  </group>

                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="A schematic cross-section of the ellipsoid together with the given plane, the two tangency points, and the gradient vectors">
                    <annotation ref="constraint"
                                text="The constraint curve g equals 0, a cross-section of the ellipsoid x squared plus y squared plus 3 z squared equals 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="plane"
                                text="The given plane x plus 5 y plus 2 z equals 3, which does not meet the ellipsoid"/>
                    <annotation ref="tangencies"
                                text="The two points on the ellipsoid where the tangent plane is parallel to the given plane">
                      <annotation ref="tangency-S1"
                                  text="At S 1, the point of the ellipsoid closest to the plane, the tangent plane is parallel to the given plane and the gradient of g is parallel to n"/>
                      <annotation ref="tangency-S2"
                                  text="At S 2, the point of the ellipsoid farthest from the plane, the tangent plane is again parallel to the given plane and the gradient of g points opposite to n"/>
                    </annotation>
                    <annotation ref="distance"
                                text="The distance from S 1 to the plane, the quantity minimized in the Lagrange multiplier method"/>
                    <annotation ref="normal"
                                text="The point P equals 3 comma 0 comma 0 chosen on the plane, with the normal vector n equals 1 comma 5 comma 2"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>

              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Choose a point on the plane, say <m>P(3,0,0)</m>.
          For a point <m>S(x,y,z)</m> on the ellipsoid, the distance from
          <m>S</m> to the plane is
          <me>
            \frac{\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\mathbf{n}}{\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert}
            = \frac{\langle x-3,\, y,\, z\rangle\cdot\langle 1,5,2\rangle}
                   {\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert}.
          </me>
          Since <m>\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert</m> is constant, it suffices to
          optimize the numerator
          <me>
            f(x,y,z) = x - 3 + 5y + 2z
          </me>
          subject to the constraint
          <me>
            g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 - 1 = 0.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla f = \lambda\, \nabla g</m> gives
          <me>
            \langle 1, 5, 2\rangle = \lambda \langle 2x,\, 2y,\, 6z\rangle,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            x = \frac{1}{2\lambda}, \qquad
            y = \frac{5}{2\lambda}, \qquad
            z = \frac{1}{3\lambda}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Substituting into the constraint <m>x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 = 1</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{1}{4\lambda^2} + \frac{25}{4\lambda^2} + \frac{3}{9\lambda^2} \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{3 + 75 + 4}{12\lambda^2} \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{1}{\lambda^2} \amp= \frac{12}{82},</mrow>
          </md>
          so that
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\lambda} = \pm 2\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            x = \pm\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}, \qquad
            y = \pm 5\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}, \qquad
            z = \pm\sqrt{\frac{2}{123}},
          </me>
          and the two points are
          <md>
            <mrow>S_1\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; 5\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; \sqrt{\tfrac{2}{123}}\right)
            \amp \qquad\text{and}</mrow>
            <mrow>S_2\!\left(-\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; -5\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; -\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{123}}\right). \amp</mrow>
          </md>
          The positive signs give <m>S_1</m>, the point closest to the plane,
          and the negative signs give <m>S_2</m>, the farthest.  At both
          points the tangent plane is parallel to the plane
          <m>x + 5y + 2z = 3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-cobb-douglas">
      <title>Maximizing a Cobb-Douglas utility</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A consumer has <dollar/>600 to spend on two commodities, the first
          of which costs <dollar/>20 per unit and the second <dollar/>30 per
          unit.  Suppose that the utility derived by the consumer from
          <m>x</m> units of the first commodity and <m>y</m> units of the
          second commodity is given by the Cobb-Douglas utility function
          <me>
            U(x,y) = 10\,x^{0.6}y^{0.4}.
          </me>
          How many units of each commodity should the consumer buy to
          maximize utility?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The budget constraint is <m>20x + 30y = 600</m>, so we take
          <me>
            g(x,y) = 20x + 30y - 600 = 0,
          </me>
          with <m>g_x = 20</m> and <m>g_y = 30</m>, and maximize
          <m>U(x,y) = 10\,x^{0.6}y^{0.4}</m>, whose partial derivatives are
          <me>
            U_x = 6\,x^{-0.4}y^{0.4}, \qquad
            U_y = 4\,x^{0.6}y^{-0.6}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla U = \lambda\,\nabla g</m> gives
          the system
          <me>
            6\,x^{-0.4}y^{0.4} = 20\lambda, \qquad
            4\,x^{0.6}y^{-0.6} = 30\lambda.
          </me>
          Dividing the first equation by the second eliminates
          <m>\lambda</m>:
          <me>
            \frac{3}{2}\cdot\frac{y}{x} = \frac{2}{3}
            \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
            y = \frac{4}{9}\,x.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Substituting into the budget constraint,
          <md>
            <mrow>20x + 30\left(\frac{4}{9}x\right) \amp= 600</mrow>
            <mrow>20x + \frac{40}{3}x \amp= 600</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{100}{3}x \amp= 600,</mrow>
          </md>
          so <m>x = 18</m> and <m>y = 18\cdot\frac{4}{9} = 8</m>.
          The consumer should buy <m>18</m> units of the first commodity and
          <m>8</m> units of the second.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-7">
<statement>
<p>
You have 24 square inches of cardboard and want to build a box in the
shape of a rectangular prism. Find the dimensions of the box, if you
would like it to have the largest possible volume. See
<xref ref="fig-rf-box"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rf-box">
<caption>
A rectangular prism with dimensions <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>.
</caption>
<image width="46%">
<description>
<p>
A rectangular box drawn in perspective, with the width labelled x on
the front left edge, the depth labelled y along the top front edge,
and the height labelled z on the left face.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.0]
  % back edges
  \draw[gray!70] (0,0,0) -- (0,3.4,0);
  \draw[gray!70] (0,0,0) -- (1.8,0,0);
  \draw[gray!70] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1.6);
  % visible edges
  \draw[thick] (1.8,0,0) -- (1.8,3.4,0) -- (0,3.4,0) -- (0,3.4,1.6) -- (0,0,1.6) -- (1.8,0,1.6) -- cycle;
  \draw[thick] (1.8,3.4,0) -- (1.8,3.4,1.6);
  \draw[thick] (1.8,0,1.6) -- (1.8,3.4,1.6) -- (0,3.4,1.6);
  % labels
  \node[below left=1pt] at (0.9,0,0) {$x$};
  \node[above=1pt] at (1.8,1.7,1.6) {$y$};
  \node[left=2pt] at (0,0,0.8) {$z$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The surface area of the box is
<md>
2xy + 2xz + 2yz = 24,
</md>
so the constraint is <m>g(x,y,z) = xy + xz + yz - 12 = 0</m>, and the
function to be maximized is <m>f(x,y,z) = xyz</m>. We compute
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = \langle f_x, f_y, f_z\rangle
= \langle yz, xz, xy\rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla g \amp = \langle y+z, x+z, x+y\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
The Lagrange system <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>yz \amp = \lambda(y+z)</mrow>
<mrow>xz \amp = \lambda(x+z)</mrow>
<mrow>xy \amp = \lambda(x+y)</mrow>
</md>
Multiplying the equations by <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>
respectively gives
<md>
xyz = \lambda x(y+z), \qquad
xyz = \lambda y(x+z), \qquad
xyz = \lambda z(x+y).
</md>
Equating the first two, <m>x(y+z) = y(x+z)</m>, which simplifies to
<m>xz = yz</m>, i.e. <m>x = y</m>. Equating the last two,
<m>y(x+z) = z(x+y)</m>, which simplifies to <m>xy = xz</m>, i.e.
<m>y = z</m>.
</p>

<p>
So <m>x = y = z</m>, and substituting into the constraint
<m>xy + xz + yz - 12 = 0</m> gives <m>3x^2 = 12</m>, or
<m>x^2 = 4</m>. Hence
<md>
x = y = z = 2 \text{ inches}
</md>
and these dimensions (a cube) will maximize the volume.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-10">
<statement>
<p>
Use the method of Lagrange Multipliers to find the maximum and
minimum values of <m>f(x,y) = \sqrt2\,x - \sqrt2\,y</m> subject to the
constraint <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 4 = 0</m>, we have
<md>
\nabla f = \sqrt2\,\mathbf i - \sqrt2\,\mathbf j,
\qquad
\nabla g = (2x)\,\mathbf i + (2y)\,\mathbf j.
</md>
The system <m>\nabla f = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>\sqrt2 \amp = 2\lambda x
\;\Rightarrow\; x = \frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda},</mrow>
<mrow>-\sqrt2 \amp = 2\lambda y
\;\Rightarrow\; y = -\frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda}.</mrow>
</md>
Substituting into the constraint,
<md>
\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda}\right)^{2}
+ \left(-\frac{1}{\sqrt2\,\lambda}\right)^{2} = 4
\;\Rightarrow\; \frac{1}{\lambda^2} = 4
\;\Rightarrow\; \lambda = \pm\frac12.
</md>
For <m>\lambda_1 = \frac12</m>: <m>x = \sqrt2</m>,
<m>y = -\sqrt2</m>. For <m>\lambda_2 = -\frac12</m>:
<m>x = -\sqrt2</m>, <m>y = \sqrt2</m>. Evaluating <m>f</m>,
<md>
<mrow>f\left(\sqrt2, -\sqrt2\right) \amp = 2 + 2 = 4
\qquad \text{absolute maximum,}</mrow>
<mrow>f\left(-\sqrt2, \sqrt2\right) \amp = -2 - 2 = -4
\qquad \text{absolute minimum.}</mrow>
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-rs19-lagrange"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rs19-lagrange">
<caption>
The constraint circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m> and the level lines
<m>\sqrt2\,x - \sqrt2\,y = \pm 4</m> of <m>f</m>, which are tangent to
the circle at the extrema
<m>\left(\sqrt2, -\sqrt2\right)</m> and
<m>\left(-\sqrt2, \sqrt2\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-rs19-lagrange">
<diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.6,-3.6,3.6,3.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="lg-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<line at="lg-max" endpoints="((-0.4,-3.25),(3.25,0.4))"
      stroke="#D55E00" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="lg-min" endpoints="((-3.25,-0.4),(0.4,3.25))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<point at="lg-pmax" p="(1.4142,-1.4142)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="lg-pmin" p="(-1.4142,1.4142)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="lg-pmax-lab" anchor="(1.55,-1.6)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>\left(\sqrt2,-\sqrt2\right)</m>
</label>
<label at="lg-pmin-lab" anchor="(-1.55,1.6)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\left(-\sqrt2,\sqrt2\right)</m>
</label>
<label at="lg-max-lab" anchor="(2.65,-0.05)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=4</m>
</label>
<label at="lg-min-lab" anchor="(-2.65,0.05)" alignment="sw" color="#009E73">
<m>f=-4</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue circle of radius two centered at the origin. A dashed vermillion line of slope one touches the circle at the point square root of two comma negative square root of two, where f equals four, and a dashed green line of slope one touches it at negative square root of two comma square root of two, where f equals negative four.">
  <annotation ref="lg-circle" text="The constraint circle x squared plus y squared equals four."/>
  <annotation ref="lg-pmax" text="The absolute maximum at square root of two comma negative square root of two, where f equals four."/>
  <annotation ref="lg-pmin" text="The absolute minimum at negative square root of two comma square root of two, where f equals negative four."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-37">
<statement>
<p>
Given <m>f(x,y) = -x^2 + 4x - y^2 - 4y</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum and minimum values of <m>f(x,y)</m> on the circle
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>, first using Lagrange multipliers, then again by
parametrizing the circle.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum and minimum values of <m>f(x,y)</m> over the disk
<m>x^2 + y^2 \le 9</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<em>Lagrange multipliers.</em> With
<m>g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 9 = 0</m>, the system
<m>\nabla f = \lambda\nabla g</m> reads
<md>
<mrow>-2x + 4 \amp = 2\lambda x \amp \;\Rightarrow\;
x \amp = \frac{2}{1+\lambda},</mrow>
<mrow>-2y - 4 \amp = 2\lambda y \amp \;\Rightarrow\;
y \amp = \frac{-2}{1+\lambda}.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>y = -x</m>, and substituting into the constraint,
<m>2x^2 = 9</m>, so <m>x = \pm\frac{3}{\sqrt2}</m> and
<m>y = \mp\frac{3}{\sqrt2}</m>. Evaluating <m>f</m> at these two
points, and noting that <m>-x^2 - y^2 = -9</m> on the circle,
<md>
f = -9 + 4x - 4y = -9 + 4(x - y) = -9 \pm \frac{24}{\sqrt2}.
</md>
So the maximum on the circle is
<m>-9 + \frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m> at
<m>\left(\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m> and the
minimum is <m>-9 - \frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m> at
<m>\left(-\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, \frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>By parametrizing.</em> On the circle,
<m>x = 3\cos t</m>, <m>y = 3\sin t</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>f \amp = -9\cos^2 t + 12\cos t - 9\sin^2 t - 12\sin t</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -9 + 12(\cos t - \sin t)
= -9 + 12\sqrt2 \cos\!\left(t + \frac{\pi}{4}\right).</mrow>
</md>
Since <m>\cos</m> ranges over <m>[-1,1]</m>, this ranges over
<m>-9 \pm 12\sqrt2 = -9 \pm \frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m>, agreeing with the
Lagrange computation.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Over the disk we must also check the interior. Setting
<md>
f_x = -2x + 4 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; x = 2,
\qquad
f_y = -2y - 4 = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; y = -2,
</md>
gives the critical point <m>(2,-2)</m>, which does lie inside the disk
since <m>4 + 4 = 8 \lt 9</m>. There
<m>f(2,-2) = -4 + 8 - 4 + 8 = 8</m>. (It is a local maximum:
<m>f_{xx} = -2 \lt 0</m>, <m>f_{yy} = -2</m>, <m>f_{xy} = 0</m>, so
<m>D = 4 \gt 0</m>.)
</p>
<p>
Comparing with the boundary values from part A, the maximum over the
disk is <m>8</m> at <m>(2,-2)</m>, and the minimum is
<m>-9 - \frac{24}{\sqrt2} \approx -25.97</m> at
<m>\left(-\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, \frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-disk"/>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-disk">
<caption>
The disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 9</m>. The maximum of <m>f</m> occurs at the
interior critical point <m>(2,-2)</m>, and the minimum at the boundary
point <m>\left(-\frac{3}{\sqrt2}, \frac{3}{\sqrt2}\right)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-disk">
<diagram dimensions="(300,290)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (3*cos(t), 3*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-5.4,-5.0,5.4,5.0)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="dk2-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dk2-max" p="(2,-2)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="dk2-min" p="(-2.1213,2.1213)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="dk2-max-lab" anchor="(2.2,-2.2)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>(2,-2),\ f=8</m>
</label>
<label at="dk2-min-lab" anchor="(-2.3,2.35)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>f=-9-\frac{24}{\sqrt2}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue circle of radius three centered at the origin. A vermillion point inside the circle at two comma negative two marks the maximum, f equals eight. A green point on the circle in the second quadrant marks the minimum.">
  <annotation ref="dk2-circle" text="The boundary circle x squared plus y squared equals nine."/>
  <annotation ref="dk2-max" text="The interior critical point two comma negative two, where the maximum eight occurs."/>
  <annotation ref="dk2-min" text="The boundary point where the minimum occurs."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-39">
<statement>
<p>
You are in charge of erecting a radio telescope on a newly discovered
planet. To minimize interference, you want to place it where the
magnetic field of the planet is weakest. The planet is spherical, with
a radius of 6 units. Based on a coordinate system whose origin is at
the center of the planet, the strength of the magnetic field is given
by
<md>
M(x,y,z) = 6x - y^2 + xz + 60.
</md>
Where should you locate the radio telescope?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We must minimize <m>M</m> subject to the constraint that we stay on
the surface of the planet, <m>g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 36</m>. The
Lagrange system <m>\nabla M = \lambda \nabla g</m> is
<md>
<mrow>6 + z \amp = 2\lambda x,</mrow>
<mrow>-2y \amp = 2\lambda y,</mrow>
<mrow>x \amp = 2\lambda z.</mrow>
</md>
The second equation factors as <m>2y(\lambda + 1) = 0</m>, so either
<m>\lambda = -1</m> or <m>y = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Case <m>\lambda = -1</m>.</em> The first and third equations
become <m>6 + z = -2x</m> and <m>x = -2z</m>. Substituting,
<m>6 + z = 4z</m>, so <m>z = 2</m> and <m>x = -4</m>. The constraint
then gives <m>16 + y^2 + 4 = 36</m>, i.e. <m>y = \pm 4</m>. At these
points
<md>
M(-4, \pm 4, 2) = -24 - 16 - 8 + 60 = 12.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Case <m>y = 0</m>.</em> From the third equation
<m>\lambda = \dfrac{x}{2z}</m> (with <m>z \ne 0</m>; if <m>z = 0</m>
then <m>x = 0</m> and the first equation gives <m>6 = 0</m>, which is
impossible). Substituting into the first,
<m>6 + z = \dfrac{x^2}{z}</m>, i.e. <m>x^2 = 6z + z^2</m>. Combined
with <m>x^2 + z^2 = 36</m> this gives
<md>
6z + z^2 = 36 - z^2
\;\Rightarrow\; 2z^2 + 6z - 36 = 0
\;\Rightarrow\; (z+6)(z-3) = 0,
</md>
so <m>z = -6</m> (giving <m>x = 0</m>, with <m>M = 60</m>) or
<m>z = 3</m> (giving <m>x = \pm 3\sqrt3</m>, with
<m>M = 60 \pm 27\sqrt3 \approx 13.2</m> or <m>106.8</m>).
</p>

<p>
Comparing all the candidate values
<m>\{12,\; 60,\; 13.2,\; 106.8\}</m>, the magnetic field is weakest
where <m>M = 12</m>. So the radio telescope should be located at
<md>
(-4, 4, 2) \qquad\text{or}\qquad (-4, -4, 2).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>